# A tour of Spatex

Run this notebook from a git checkout of Spatex: it installs Spatex from the checkout (`path:`) and reads its test fixtures.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:spatex, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:rustler, "~> 0.38", runtime: false},
  {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
])
```

## What this notebook does

Spatex is PySAL-style spatial analysis for Elixir. This tour walks the usual exploratory workflow on
one dataset: **spatial weights**, **global and local autocorrelation**, **hot spots with a false
discovery rate**, **map classification**, a **point-pattern** quick look and **OLS with spatial
diagnostics**.

The data is the classic Columbus, Ohio neighbourhood crime dataset (49 polygons), bundled with the
repository as a test fixture, so the notebook runs offline. Every random step takes a `seed:`; the
same seed always gives the same result, whatever the number of threads. The first run compiles the
Rust core (a few minutes in debug mode).

```elixir
fixture = Path.join([__DIR__, "..", "test", "reference", "fixtures", "datasets", "columbus.json"])
columbus = fixture |> File.read!() |> Jason.decode!()

wkbs = Enum.map(columbus["wkb_hex"], &Base.decode16!(&1, case: :mixed))
crime = columbus["attributes"]["CRIME"]
income = columbus["attributes"]["INC"]
hoval = columbus["attributes"]["HOVAL"]

Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.zip_with([crime, income, hoval], fn [c, i, h] -> %{crime: c, income: i, house_value: h} end)
)
```

## Spatial weights

A `Spatex.Graph` records who is a neighbour of whom. `build_contiguity/2` reads polygon WKB; with
`rook: false` two polygons are neighbours when they share at least a vertex (queen contiguity).
Weights start binary; row-standardizing (`:r`) makes each row sum to one, so that the spatial lag
`W y` is the *average* of the neighbours. Pass the graph as built: the ESDA functions apply
`transform: :r` themselves.

```elixir
queen = Spatex.Graph.build_contiguity(wkbs, rook: false)

%{
  sites: Spatex.Graph.n(queen),
  links: Spatex.Graph.nnz(queen),
  islands: Spatex.Graph.isolates(queen),
  neighbours_per_site: queen |> Spatex.Graph.cardinalities() |> Enum.sum() |> Kernel./(49)
}
```

```elixir
row_standardized = Spatex.Graph.transform(queen, :r)
lag = Spatex.Graph.lag(row_standardized, crime)
Enum.zip(crime, lag) |> Enum.take(5)
```

## Global Moran's I

Moran's I summarises spatial autocorrelation in one number: positive when similar values cluster,
negative when they alternate. Inference is analytic (`p_norm`, `p_rand`) and by permutation
(`p_sim`): shuffle the values over the sites `permutations` times and see how extreme the observed
statistic is.

```elixir
moran = Spatex.ESDA.moran(crime, queen, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)

%{i: moran.i, expected: moran.ei, z: moran.z_norm, p_analytic: moran.p_norm, p_sim: moran.p_sim}
```

Crime is strongly clustered: I is about 0.50 against an expectation of -0.02, and with 999
permutations the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.001. The result also records the seed, so the
run can be repeated exactly.

## Local Moran's I (LISA)

`moran_local/3` decomposes the global statistic into one value per site and puts each site in a
quadrant of the Moran scatterplot (1 High-High, 2 Low-High, 3 Low-Low, 4 High-Low). `cluster_labels/2`
names the quadrant only where the pseudo p-value is below the significance level, and calls the other
sites "Insignificant". A *High-High* site is a high value among high neighbours (a cluster); a
*High-Low* site is a high value among low neighbours (an outlier).

```elixir
lisa = Spatex.ESDA.moran_local(crime, queen, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)
labels = Spatex.ESDA.MoranLocal.cluster_labels(lisa, 0.05)

labels |> Enum.frequencies() |> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 1), :desc)
```

```elixir
labels
|> Enum.with_index()
|> Enum.reject(fn {label, _} -> label == "Insignificant" end)
|> Enum.map(fn {label, site} ->
  %{site: site, label: label, crime: Enum.at(crime, site), p_sim: Enum.at(lisa.p_sim, site)}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

Testing 49 sites at the 5% level will flag about two by chance alone; see the next section for how to
correct for that.

## Hot spots with G_Local and FDR

`g_local/3` is the Getis-Ord statistic: with `star: true` (G*) each site's own value is included.
A large positive z-score is a hot spot, a large negative one a cold spot. Because many sites are
tested at once, `fdr/2` gives the false-discovery-rate cut-off (a Benjamini-Hochberg style
threshold): keep the sites whose `p_sim` is below it.

```elixir
gi = Spatex.ESDA.g_local(crime, queen, star: true, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)
cutoff = Spatex.ESDA.fdr(gi.p_sim, 0.05)

classify = fn z, p, threshold ->
  cond do
    p >= threshold -> "not significant"
    z > 0 -> "hot spot"
    true -> "cold spot"
  end
end

at_5_percent = Enum.zip_with(gi.zs, gi.p_sim, &classify.(&1, &2, 0.05))
after_fdr = Enum.zip_with(gi.zs, gi.p_sim, &classify.(&1, &2, cutoff))

%{
  fdr_cutoff: cutoff,
  at_5_percent: Enum.frequencies(at_5_percent),
  after_fdr: Enum.frequencies(after_fdr)
}
```

The FDR cut-off is stricter than 0.05, so fewer sites survive: those are the ones to report.

## Classification

`Spatex.Classify` mirrors `mapclassify`. Fisher-Jenks picks the `k` classes that minimise the
within-class variance, which suits skewed variables better than equal intervals. The result holds
the bins (upper bounds), the counts per class, the class of each site (`yb`) and goodness-of-fit
measures.

```elixir
jenks = Spatex.Classify.fisher_jenks(crime, k: 5)
quantiles = Spatex.Classify.quantiles(crime, k: 5)

%{
  fisher_jenks: %{bins: jenks.bins, counts: jenks.counts, gadf: jenks.gadf},
  quantiles: %{bins: quantiles.bins, counts: quantiles.counts, gadf: quantiles.gadf}
}
```

```elixir
Spatex.Classify.legend(jenks)
```

`gadf` (goodness of absolute deviation of fit) is higher for Fisher-Jenks because it optimises that
criterion.

## Point patterns

`Spatex.PointPats` looks at the locations themselves. Here we simulate a clustered and a random
pattern in the same window and compare their nearest-neighbour distance functions (`G`) against
complete spatial randomness (CSR) with a seeded Monte Carlo envelope. For a clustered pattern, `G`
rises faster than expected, so more of its distances sit above the upper envelope.

```elixir
window = {0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 100.0}
clustered = Spatex.PointPats.cluster_poisson(window, size: 100, n_seeds: 5, cluster_radius: 6.0, seed: 3)
random = Spatex.PointPats.poisson(window, size: 100, seed: 3)

summary = fn points ->
  test = Spatex.PointPats.g_test(points, permutations: 199, seed: 1)

  %{
    mean_center: Spatex.PointPats.mean_center(points),
    std_distance: Spatex.PointPats.std_distance(points),
    distances_above_envelope:
      test.statistic |> Enum.zip(test.envelope_high) |> Enum.count(fn {g, hi} -> g > hi end),
    distances_tested: length(test.support)
  }
end

%{clustered: summary.(clustered), random: summary.(random)}
```

```elixir
quadrat = Spatex.PointPats.quadrat_test(clustered, nx: 4, ny: 4)
%{chi2: quadrat.chi2, df: quadrat.df, p_value: quadrat.chi2_pvalue}
```

A quadrat test on the clustered pattern rejects an even spread of points over the 4 x 4 grid.

## OLS with spatial diagnostics

`Spatex.Regression.ols/3` fits least squares with `spreg`'s diagnostics. Given a (row-standardized)
graph it adds the Lagrange multiplier tests for a missing spatial lag or spatial error, and Moran's I
of the residuals. Spatex never transforms the graph behind your back, so standardize it first.

```elixir
model =
  Spatex.Regression.ols(crime, %{"INC" => income, "HOVAL" => hoval},
    name_y: "CRIME",
    graph: row_standardized,
    spat_diag: true,
    moran: true
  )

IO.puts(Spatex.Regression.summary(model))

Enum.zip([model.name_x, model.betas, model.std_err])
|> Enum.map(fn {name, beta, se} -> %{variable: name, coefficient: beta, std_error: se} end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

```elixir
%{
  lm_lag: model.lm_lag,
  lm_error: model.lm_error,
  robust_lm_lag: model.rlm_lag,
  robust_lm_error: model.rlm_error,
  moran_of_residuals: model.moran_res
}
```

Each diagnostic is `{statistic, p_value}`. A small p-value for the lag or error test means the
residuals still carry spatial structure, which suggests a spatial lag or error model rather than OLS.
Look at the robust versions to decide between the two.

